---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="mhs — Examples"
  description="Move three slices through the field of a surface-breaking fault in a mollified Mindlin half space, and through the difference against the same fault in a full space."
  current="examples"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>Examples<span class="sub">what the free surface is worth</span></h1>
    <p>
      The same fault as on the front page &mdash; 40&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;20&nbsp;km,
      dipping 60&deg;, breaking the surface, 1&nbsp;m of uniform strike slip
      &mdash; evaluated on a grid through the half space and read back three
      slices at a time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two states and their difference. <b>Half space</b> is this kernel.
      <b>Full space</b> is the same fault with the Mindlin image terms removed,
      which is what you get from a classical triangular dislocation element in an
      infinite medium. The difference is the free surface, by itself.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      At the free surface the half-space displacement is a median
      <b>2.01&times;</b> the full-space one. That factor is why
      <code>disp_matrix</code> cannot be short-circuited to the full-space
      answer: the error would be the entire free-surface effect, which is
      exactly the size that looks plausible.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      The sampled box reaches above <i>z</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0. Those points are
      outside the body and carry no data, so the body visibly ends at the grey
      grid, which is the free surface.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="state"><span>state</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="field"><span>field</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="map"><span>colours</span></label>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="Three movable orthogonal slices through the stress and displacement field of a surface-breaking fault in an elastic half space"></div>
    <div data-bar class="bar"></div>
    <div class="panel slices">
      <span class="lbl">slices</span>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="slice-x"><span>x</span></div>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="slice-y"><span>y</span></div>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="slice-z"><span>z</span></div>
    </div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading…</p>
  </div>

  <p class="cap">
    <b>Every point inside the half space is drawn</b>, including those nearest
    the fault where the field is largest and where a quadrature scheme is most
    strained. Nothing is masked out, because the two states differ only in
    whether the free surface is present &mdash; so hiding a region would hide
    exactly the comparison this page is for. Colour is on a log scale; the
    difference state shows the magnitude of a signed difference.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    The field is stored quantised: log<sub>10</sub> into one byte per grid point,
    254 levels, with zero reserved for &ldquo;outside the body&rdquo;. That is
    not for file size &mdash; a float32 3-D texture simply cannot be linearly
    filtered on roughly half of iOS devices, and it fails by rendering black with
    no error raised. The quantisation error is bounded by arithmetic rather than
    asserted: 254 levels across <i>d</i> decades put the levels
    <i>d</i>/253 apart, so the worst relative error is
    10<sup><i>d</i>/506</sup>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;1. The build measures what it
    wrote against that bound and refuses to publish a payload that exceeds it.
    See <a href={`${base}concepts/`}>Concepts</a> for what the eigenstress
    subtraction means, and <a href={`${base}running/`}>Running</a> for the calls
    that produced this.
  </p>

  <script>
    import { startViewer } from "../viz/controls";
    startViewer(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
